 User: Consider the curve $x=7\sec(\theta)$.  How many times does this curve meet the line $y=x+100$? Alternate Solution.  The tangent of $\theta$ is the derivative of the right side of the equation for $x$, $x=7\sec(\theta)$.  That derivative, $7\sec(\theta) \tan(\theta)$, represents the slope of the curve.  The slope of the line $y=x+100$ is one.  For their curves to intersect, the slopes must be equal, which is the equation
\[7\sec(\theta)\tan(\theta)=1.\]
Noting that \[\frac{d}{d\theta}(\sec(\theta))=\sec(\theta)\tan(\theta),\] our equation can be written as \[7\frac{d}{d\theta}(\sec(\theta))=1.\]  Likewise, \[\frac{d_{x}}{d_{\theta}}\left(7\sin(\pi/ \theta)\right)=1/ \theta^{2}.\]  These two equations are not the same, however, for the second one takes the negative argument, and the condition that $\sin(\pi/\theta)$ is under the square root sign implies that it is actually the cosinus of an angle.  Because of this difference, the curves intersect twice—once in the $x$-nrst quadrant and again in the $x$-nd quadrant.

